A1. What golfers do the rest of their lives after making a hole in one. ~ BRAG
A5. These people have lots of sand. ~ ARABS
A8. Pertaining to the air. ~ AERO
A12. These are the kind of girls that men forget. ~ LEMONS
A14. What husbands should do when their wives speak. ~ HARKEN
A16. Where umbrellas go when it rains. ~ UP
A17. A person who gives orders. (This has nothing to do with wives or mother-in-laws). ~ BOSS
A19. This was invented to keep out honest people; crooks know how to open 'em. ~ LOCK
A20. Faded Cucumbers (abbr.). ~ FC
A21. A long time. ~ EON
A23. To promise to love, honor and obey, but not for long. ~ BETROTH
A25. A Chinaman makes a good actor because he never forgets this. ~ CUE
A26. Something a peddler does when he has some bananas, besides singing about it. ~ SELLS
A28. "The skin you love to touch" comes from these. (Ask any chorus girl.) ~ SEALS
A30. A party in Herrion, Ill. ~ MELEE
A31. Something a dumb-bell wears over the weak end. ~ HAT
A33. A difficult thing to do in a sleeper. ~ SLEEP
A35. Lettuce Salad or Lettuce Sleep (abbr.). ~ LS
A36. What marriage does to wild women; but not much. ~ TAMES
A38. The poor Indian. ~ LO
A39. Where candidates go, when they're elected. ~ IN
A40. What people don't like to be when they're in a bathtub. ~ SEEN
A41. The name of a maid. ~ NORA
A42. The pronoun of the first person. ~ ME
A43. A note that sounds like money. ~ DO
A44. What the hole in a doughnut is. ~ EDGED
A46. What people should do after they make up their minds to do something. ~ DO
A48. The part of an argument that should be followed; but not with bricks. ~ TREND
A51. A member of the floating population. ~ SOT
A52. What a man does when a bill collector pays him a visit. ~ HIDES
A54. A wet spot in a dry country. ~ OASIS
A56. What Arabs live on (pl.). ~ SANDS
A57. This place is a long way off. ~ FAR
A58. High-brow flappers. ~ MAIDENS
A61. A spring of mineral water. ~ SPA
A63. This comes before noon. ~ AM
A64. What the loss of our straw hats will soon be. ~ FELT
A65. This is a difficult thing to meet. ~ NOTE
A67. Two eyes. (Super super simplified spelling.) ~ II
A68. Something that it's useless to do when friend wife makes up her mind. ~ RESIST
A71. A bookkeeper isn't necessarily one of these. ~ READER
A73. The home of cops and union bricklayers. ~ ERIN
A74. This should be wiped clean. ~ SLATE
A75. This is what they do to the bull in both Spain and America. ~ TOSS

D1. What men get, when their Sweetie goes away. ~ BLUE
D2. This is found in the arms of Morpheus. ~ REPOSE
D3. What flappers come home from dances in. ~ AM
D4. This fellow plays cards and sits on the deck. ~ GOB
D5. What Bryan is proving that men are descendants of. ~ ASSES
D6. Preposition of place. ~ AT
D7. These come from the hip. ~ SHOTS
D8. The boat that was conceived by disbelief in the song "It ain't gonna rain no mo'!" ~ ARK
D9. Electrified kicks (abbr.). ~ EK
D10. No woman can do this when she's offered a bargain. ~ REFUSE
D11. Bigamy is when you get married twice; monotony is when you get married this number of times. ~ ONCE
D13. The kind of ambitions good office boys have. ~ NOBLE
D15. A head that's just been crowned does this. ~ ACHES
D18. An abbreviation for something secondhand cars run on. ~ ST
D19. That poor Indian again. ~ LO
D22. Nellie the beautiful cloak model. ~ NELL
D24. People who have Roman noses don't necessarily do this. ~ ROAM
D25. Mark's lady friend. ~ CLEO
D27. Things people learn from buying secondhand cars. ~ LESSONS
D29. This man could do more with his one lamp than Argus could with his hundred eyes. ~ ALLADIN
D30. When anything gets this way it's all wet. ~ MOIST
D31. Things that bridge players play with. ~ HANDS
D32. A doctrine or creed. ~ TENET
D34. These are things that people slip on easily. (No, Dora, not kimona!) ~ PEELS
D36. A hard thing to get away from on a golf course. ~ TEE
D37. Something that reckless drivers hurry to get under. ~ SOD
D43. This is expensive. ~ DEAR
D45. These kind of people die young. (Pedestrian is a ten-letter word!) ~ GOOD
D47. These are against you when you play poker with a professional. ~ ODDS
D49. A wanderer. (We wander if you can get it!) ~ ROAMER
D50. The Woolworth Building was built on these. ~ DIMES
D52. This makes waste. ~ HASTE
D53. Discovers unexpectedly. ~ ESPIES
D55. Old sailors. ~ SALTS
D56. This is known as sheet music. ~ SNORE
D57. This is what the conductor said it wasn't as he returned the button. ~ FARE
D59. A pronoun. ~ IT
D60. Half of a printer's measure. ~ EN
D62. This is what most women put on when they go horseback riding. ~ AIRS
D64. A fish's wing. ~ FIN
D66. Do this, drink and be merry for to-morrow all three may be illegal. ~ EAT
D69. A Spanish "yes." ~ SI
D70. Maker of old-fashioned pies. ~ MA
D72. The first note in the diatonic scale. ~ DO


